1
00:00:00,418 --> 00:00:03,093
Let’s compare two models on the same
bug.

2
00:00:03,143 --> 00:00:05,543
The baseline function accepts zero.

3
00:00:05,593 --> 00:00:09,728
The issue says one and five should pass,
while zero and negative one should fail.

4
00:00:10,331 --> 00:00:13,931
We ask for a cause, a small fix, and one
regression test.

5
00:00:13,981 --> 00:00:17,721
Before opening either chat, write these
expectations down.

6
00:00:17,796 --> 00:00:20,019
That gives both answers the same
standard.

7
00:00:21,476 --> 00:00:24,251
Open the model picker in Copilot Chat.

8
00:00:24,301 --> 00:00:28,251
Choose two options actually available in
your managed workspace and record their

9
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:28,806
labels.

10
00:00:29,453 --> 00:00:31,928
Use the same session mode for both.

11
00:00:32,003 --> 00:00:35,828
If your account offers one model, run it
twice to see whether the answer stays

12
00:00:35,878 --> 00:00:36,630
consistent.

13
00:00:37,286 --> 00:00:39,965
Record those as two attempts with the
same model.

14
00:00:41,481 --> 00:00:44,431
Use two fresh Plan sessions on the same
revision.

15
00:00:44,506 --> 00:00:48,141
In each, attach the issue, the
validator, and its tests.

16
00:00:48,216 --> 00:00:49,387
Paste the same prompt.

17
00:00:50,015 --> 00:00:55,140
Ask why zero passes, then request a
minimal fix and one regression test.

18
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:57,465
Leave edits out of this comparison.

19
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:00,490
If a run sees more source or uses a
different mode,

20
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:03,208
record that difference before
interpreting its result.

21
00:01:04,706 --> 00:01:08,381
Example A identifies equality, proposes
a zero test,

22
00:01:08,521 --> 00:01:10,721
and keeps the existing interface.

23
00:01:10,821 --> 00:01:15,497
Example B also finds the cause, but adds
a refactor the issue never asked for.

24
00:01:16,098 --> 00:01:17,798
Both may sound polished.

25
00:01:17,873 --> 00:01:19,623
Score the substance first.

26
00:01:19,698 --> 00:01:22,548
Only then look at the example response
times.

27
00:01:22,623 --> 00:01:26,098
Those numbers describe these
illustrative runs and can vary across

28
00:01:26,148 --> 00:01:27,268
attempts and sessions.

29
00:01:28,768 --> 00:01:33,218
For this task, the smaller proposal is
easier to review because the expected

30
00:01:33,268 --> 00:01:35,343
behavior is explicit.

31
00:01:35,418 --> 00:01:39,368
A larger migration may justify
evaluating a stronger reasoning option

32
00:01:39,418 --> 00:01:40,834
on a representative task.

33
00:01:41,446 --> 00:01:45,621
You can make that choice from several
runs and the evidence they produce.

34
00:01:45,696 --> 00:01:49,821
In this small exercise, the best signal
is whether the suggested regression

35
00:01:49,896 --> 00:01:51,016
catches the defect.

36
00:01:52,476 --> 00:01:54,476
Compare two test ideas.

37
00:01:54,551 --> 00:01:58,401
A test that expects one to pass stays
green on the buggy revision.

38
00:01:58,476 --> 00:02:02,216
A test that expects zero to fail exposes
the equality bug.

39
00:02:02,844 --> 00:02:06,744
Run the chosen assertion on the original
code in an approved runner.

40
00:02:06,819 --> 00:02:09,669
Then apply the small fix and repeat the
test.

41
00:02:09,769 --> 00:02:13,818
Seeing red first and green afterward
makes the model comparison concrete.

42
00:02:15,331 --> 00:02:18,181
Write a short decision a teammate can
repeat.

43
00:02:18,256 --> 00:02:21,421
Name the two model labels, the task, the
shared inputs,

44
00:02:21,576 --> 00:02:22,803
and the evidence you used.

45
00:02:23,437 --> 00:02:26,787
You might say Example A was easier to
review because it preserved the

46
00:02:26,837 --> 00:02:30,340
signature and produced a test that
failed on the original.

47
00:02:30,964 --> 00:02:35,564
Repeat the comparison on another small
task before treating one result as a

48
00:02:35,614 --> 00:02:36,694
general preference.
